  "answer_to_user": "Here are some big, well-established companies that currently look reasonably-priced, not stretched. \"Not too expensive\" here means the price you pay for each dollar of the company's earnings (the P/E ratio) is BELOW the average of its large-cap peers. Examples from the screen: Alphabet/Google (GOOG) trades around 17x earnings vs a peer average near 27x, plus Amazon (AMZN), Meta (META), Berkshire Hathaway (BRK-B), JPMorgan Chase (JPM), Bank of America (BAC), Exxon Mobil (XOM), Chevron (CVX), UnitedHealth (UNH) and Oracle (ORCL). All are genuinely large, household-name businesses. A lower P/E just means the market is asking less for the earnings today — it does NOT guarantee the stock is 'safe' or will go up, and a low multiple can also signal slower expected growth. This is information to research further, not a recommendation to buy."
